The name Joshua, as a corruption of the Jews, (certainly after the time of Josephus, but prior to Jerome,   who   once   mentions   it;
cf. Com. in Os. I. 1.,) ought to be everywhere rejected; but the ΝΑΓΗ of the LXX. is an error of the copyists for
ΝΑΓΝ. The Masorites have twice punctuated the name correctly in the case of Jeshua, the son of Jozadak.   (Ez. ii. 2., iii. 2.)
